/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Stoicism and philosophy */
WB.L("stoic-founders", "philosophy", "beginner", "Zeno and the painted porch",
  "A shipwrecked merchant walked into an Athens bookshop, and a philosophy was born that would guide emperors and slaves alike.",
  [
    ["Shipwreck and porch", "Zeno lost his fortune in a shipwreck, turned to philosophy, and taught in a painted colonnade that gave Stoicism its name.",
      "Zeno was a merchant from Citium, on Cyprus. According to the biographer Diogenes Laertius, a shipwreck near Athens, traditionally dated around 312 BC, cost him a cargo of purple dye. In an Athens bookshop he heard about Socrates and asked where men like that could be found. The bookseller pointed to the philosopher Crates and said, 'Follow that man.' Zeno later joked, 'I made a prosperous voyage when I was shipwrecked.' The details may be embroidered, but the moral stuck: disaster can start something better.\n\nAround 300 BC, Zeno began teaching in the Stoa Poikile, the Painted Porch, a public colonnade by the Athenian marketplace. His followers became known as Stoics, 'the porch people'."],
    ["Cynics and virtue", "From the Cynics, Zeno took the idea that virtue is enough for a good life, and the Stoics made virtue the only true good.",
      "Crates was a Cynic, one of Athens' most provocative groups. Their best-known figure, Diogenes, reportedly lived in a large storage jar, owned almost nothing and mocked wealth and fame. Crates gave away his fortune to live likewise. From them Zeno learned that happiness doesn't depend on possessions or status. The Stoics kept the core but dropped the shock tactics: you could live virtuously while marrying, working and taking part in public life.\n\nThe Stoics said the only true good is virtue: wisdom, justice, courage and self-control. Health, wealth, reputation and even life are 'indifferent'. Not worthless: they are 'preferred indifferents', sensible to pursue, but they do not decide whether your life goes well. What matters is how you use what you're dealt."],
    ["Nature and emotions", "Stoics aimed to live by reason and in community, and to correct the mistaken judgements behind destructive emotions.",
      "Zeno taught that the goal is to live in agreement with nature. The universe is ordered by reason, which Stoics called logos. Humans are rational, social beings, so living naturally meant using reason well and serving the human community. Stoics were early cosmopolitans, 'citizens of the world', picturing concern spreading outward from self and family to all of humanity.\n\nA common myth is that Stoics wanted to feel nothing. They targeted the passions: fear, craving, distress and excessive pleasure, which grow from false judgements, such as thinking a loss of money is a genuine catastrophe. Correct the judgement and the passion weakens. They welcomed good feelings such as joy and goodwill."],
    ["Building the system", "Cleanthes and Chrysippus built Stoicism into a full system of logic, physics and ethics.",
      "Zeno's successor Cleanthes, a former boxer, reportedly carried water at night to pay for his studies. He wrote the Hymn to Zeus, one of the few early Stoic works to survive in full. The third head, Chrysippus, was so influential that a saying went, 'If there had been no Chrysippus, there would have been no Stoa.' Ancient sources credit him with more than 700 works, nearly all lost.\n\nStoic philosophy had three parts: logic (how to reason), physics (how the world works) and ethics (how to live). They compared it to a field: logic is the fence, physics the soil, ethics the fruit."],
    ["Stoicism in Rome", "Rome's love of duty and self-control made Stoicism popular, and the best-known Stoic writings are Roman.",
      "In 155 BC Athens sent three philosophers to Rome as ambassadors, including the Stoic Diogenes of Babylon, and their lectures caused a sensation. Stoicism's focus on duty, self-control and public service suited Roman values. Cato the Younger, who resisted Julius Caesar, became a Stoic hero.\n\nThe best-known surviving writings are from the Roman era: Seneca's letters, the teachings of Epictetus, recorded by his student Arrian, and the private notebook of Emperor Marcus Aurelius. After about AD 200 the school faded, but its ideas lived on in Christian thought, a Renaissance revival and today's popular books, podcasts and cognitive therapies."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Zeno mean by joking that he had made a prosperous voyage when shipwrecked?", ["He sold his cargo for a handsome profit","Losing his cargo led him to philosophy","He had planned the wreck to escape debt","He found treasure washed up on the shore"], 1, "Misfortune can open a better path."],
    [1, "According to the Stoics, what is the only true good?", ["Virtue","Wealth","Pleasure","Fame"], 0, "Health, wealth and reputation are 'indifferent': preferable, but they do not make a life good."],
    [2, "What did Stoics think about emotions?", ["All feelings must be suppressed completely","Emotions cannot be changed by thinking","Only joy should be avoided by the wise","Destructive ones come from false judgements"], 3, "They aimed to remove destructive passions, not feeling itself, by correcting mistaken beliefs."],
    [3, "In the Stoic picture of philosophy as a field, what was ethics?", ["The fence","The soil","The fruit","The harvest tools"], 2, "Logic guards, physics nourishes, and ethics, how to live, is the result."],
    [4, "Why was Stoicism so popular in Rome?", ["It rejected public life and politics","Its focus on duty suited Roman values","It was made the official state religion","It forbade service in the Roman army"], 1, "Stoic ideals matched Roman ideals, and Roman writers gave us the best-known texts."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A shipwreck, a bookshop and a joke about luck. How Stoicism began."});

WB.L("epictetus", "philosophy", "beginner", "Epictetus: freedom in the mind",
  "Born enslaved, lame in one leg, exiled by an emperor. Epictetus taught that no one can take away your freedom to choose your response.",
  [
    ["Slave to teacher", "Epictetus was born enslaved, gained his freedom and taught philosophy, first in Rome and then in Nicopolis.",
      "Epictetus was born around AD 50 in Hierapolis, in what is now Turkey, and enslaved from childhood. His name means 'acquired'. His owner, Epaphroditus, a secretary to Emperor Nero, allowed him to study Stoicism under Musonius Rufus. He walked with a limp. One story says his master twisted his leg until it broke while he calmly warned him it would; another says illness. After gaining his freedom, he taught in Rome.\n\nAround AD 93, Emperor Domitian expelled philosophers from Rome, and Epictetus opened a school at Nicopolis in Greece. He lived simply: when a thief stole his iron lamp, he reportedly replaced it with a clay one. He wrote nothing. His student Arrian recorded his talks in the Discourses and the short Enchiridion, or handbook."],
    ["Control and judgement", "Focus on what is up to you, and remember that your judgements, not events, disturb you.",
      "The Enchiridion opens with its central idea. Within our control are our judgements, intentions, desires and actions. Outside it are our bodies, property, reputation, other people and events. Suffering comes from treating what we can't control as ours to command, like demanding traffic clear. This isn't passivity: you still prepare and act, but you judge yourself by effort and character, not outcome.\n\nEpictetus taught that 'it is not things that disturb us, but our judgements about things.' If someone insults you, your interpretation of the insult hurts. This idea inspired Albert Ellis and Aaron Beck as they developed rational emotive behaviour therapy and cognitive therapy. Cognitive behavioural therapy, which grew from their work, is now among the most widely used talking therapies."],
    ["Training and roles", "Train your desires, actions and judgements, and play your life's roles well.",
      "Epictetus said anyone who wants to be good must train in three areas. The first is desire: wanting only what is up to us, so we are never frustrated or afraid. The second is action: carrying out our duties to others thoughtfully and fairly. The third is assent: not accepting any impression as true until we've examined it, especially when tired, angry or excited.\n\nHe compared life to a play: you don't choose your role, but you can play it well. Our duties come from our relationships as son, brother, citizen, neighbour or friend. If your brother wrongs you, remember your role as a brother and act well anyway. His philosophy was less abstract theory than how to behave at dinner, at work and in a family argument."],
    ["Handbook images", "The Enchiridion uses short images to teach acceptance of loss and moderation.",
      "The Enchiridion is full of memorable images. Never say about anything, 'I have lost it,' Epictetus advised, but rather, 'I have given it back': possessions and even people are on loan to us. Behave in life as at a banquet: when a dish comes round, take a modest portion politely; if it hasn't reached you yet, don't reach for it. And don't ask for events to happen as you wish, but wish for them to happen as they do, and your life will go smoothly. These lines are demanding, but they aim at a calm that doesn't depend on luck."],
    ["Freedom within", "Real freedom is freedom of the mind, which no tyrant can take away.",
      "For Epictetus, a formerly enslaved man, freedom meant something deep. A tyrant could chain your leg, he said, but not even Zeus could overpower your will.\n\nCenturies later, US Navy pilot James Stockdale was shot down over North Vietnam in 1965. He'd been introduced to the Enchiridion while studying at Stanford, and he credited it with helping him endure more than seven years as a prisoner of war, including torture and long periods in solitary confinement. As he parachuted down, he later wrote, he told himself he was leaving the world of technology and entering the world of Epictetus."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Epictetus's life show about his philosophy?", ["Only the free can become truly wise","Wealth is needed for a virtuous life","Wisdom comes only through public office","Mental freedom survives any circumstances"], 3, "A man born enslaved became a respected teacher, living what he taught."],
    [1, "Which of these is within your control, according to Epictetus?", ["Your reputation","Other people's behaviour","Your own judgements and choices","Whether the traffic clears"], 2, "Only our judgements, intentions and actions are truly ours; the rest is outside our control."],
    [2, "What should you do with a first impression, according to Epictetus's discipline of assent?", ["Act on it straight away","Examine it before trusting it","Ignore every impression you have","Ask other people to judge it"], 1, "Pausing to test your reactions stops anger and fear from running your decisions."],
    [3, "What did Epictetus advise saying instead of 'I have lost it'?", ["I have given it back","It was never mine","It will return","Fate took it"], 0, "Seeing possessions and people as on loan makes loss easier to accept."],
    [4, "Why did Epictetus think no tyrant could take away your freedom?", ["Tyrants cannot make or enforce laws","Freedom is guaranteed by the law","Only the gods can imprison people","Bodies can be bound, but not the will"], 3, "Freedom lies in how you choose to respond, which no one else can seize."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A man born enslaved taught that nobody can take his freedom. How did that help a POW?"});

WB.L("seneca", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Seneca: time, anger and wealth",
  "Playwright, statesman, millionaire, adviser to Nero. Seneca wrote brilliantly about living well, and struggled to live up to it.",
  [
    ["A flawed Stoic", "Seneca was a Roman philosopher and adviser to Nero who admitted falling short of his own ideals.",
      "Lucius Annaeus Seneca (about 4 BC to AD 65) was born in Córdoba, Spain, and became a leading Roman writer, orator and senator. In AD 41 Emperor Claudius exiled him to Corsica, accused of adultery with the emperor's niece, and he spent eight years there. Recalled in 49, he became tutor and then chief adviser to the young Nero. For several years he helped steer the empire, and he grew enormously rich. Critics, then and now, call him a hypocrite: a Stoic preaching simplicity while living in luxury and serving a tyrant. Seneca himself admitted he fell far short of his ideals."],
    ["Time and letters", "Life is not short; we waste much of it, and Seneca's letters to Lucilius offer practical guidance on living well.",
      "In On the Shortness of Life, Seneca argued: 'It is not that we have a short time to live, but that we waste a lot of it.' People guard their money carefully but give their time to anyone who asks. They postpone living until retirement, as if they were guaranteed to reach it. His remedy: treat time as your most precious possession, spend it deliberately and live fully today.\n\nLate in life, Seneca wrote 124 surviving letters to his friend Lucilius, a Roman official in Sicily. Each tackles a practical theme: friendship, grief, fear, old age, wealth, even noisy neighbours. They are personal and warm, often starting with something from his day. They read like advice from a wise, flawed friend."],
    ["On anger", "Seneca called anger a brief madness and gave practical ways to prevent it.",
      "In On Anger, Seneca called anger a brief insanity that harms the angry person more than its target. His strategies sound strikingly modern. Delay your response, since time cools anger. Notice the early warning signs. Avoid provoking people and situations when you're tired or hungry. Imagine how you look when furious. And consider that the other person may not have meant harm, or that you've done the same yourself.\n\nHe praised Plato, who, furious with a slave, asked his nephew Speusippus to deal with the man instead, because he was angry, and so in no state to judge."],
    ["Poverty and grief", "Practising simple living removes fear of loss, and grief is natural but should not be endless.",
      "In one letter, Seneca advised setting aside a few days now and then to live on the cheapest food and roughest clothing, then asking, 'Is this what I feared?' The point was freedom, not self-punishment. Once you know you can cope with little, the fear of losing things loosens, and you can enjoy what you have without clinging to it. Modern versions include a week of simple meals or a spending freeze.\n\nSeneca also wrote consolations, comforting the bereaved. One went to Marcia, still mourning her son three years on. Another went to his mother, Helvia, from exile. He did not urge suppressing grief: our eyes should be neither dry nor overflowing. We may weep, but not wail. Be grateful for the time you had, and gradually return to life."],
    ["Plays and a Stoic death", "Seneca wrote influential tragedies and science, then died calmly after Nero ordered him to kill himself.",
      "Seneca also wrote tragedies, including Medea, Phaedra, Oedipus and Thyestes, full of revenge and overwhelming passion, perhaps showing what happens when emotions go unchecked. They influenced Elizabethan dramatists such as Thomas Kyd and William Shakespeare. His Natural Questions investigated earthquakes, comets and thunder. For Seneca, studying nature lifted the mind above petty worries.\n\nIn AD 65 Nero accused Seneca of involvement in the Pisonian conspiracy and ordered him to take his own life. According to the historian Tacitus, he faced it calmly, told weeping friends to remember the philosophy they had studied and left them 'the image of his life'. His composure became a famous model of Stoic courage."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do critics call Seneca a hypocrite?", ["He taught Stoicism but never wrote it down","He preached simplicity yet lived in luxury","He refused to advise Nero on any matter","He lived in poverty while praising wealth"], 1, "Seneca admitted he fell far short of the ideals he taught."],
    [1, "What did Seneca say about the shortness of life?", ["Life is long enough if well used","Life is too short for planning","Only the rich have enough time","Time can be saved for later"], 0, "The problem is not the supply of time but how carelessly we spend it."],
    [2, "Which of these did Seneca recommend for dealing with anger?", ["Reply immediately while it is fresh","Suppress it completely and say nothing","Confront the person when you are tired","Delay your reply and let time cool it"], 3, "Pausing gives judgement time to return and prevents regret."],
    [3, "Why did Seneca recommend practising poverty?", ["To punish himself for his riches","To save money","To lose the fear of losing wealth","To impress others"], 2, "Learning you can cope with little loosens fear and lets you enjoy what you have."],
    [4, "What made Seneca's death a model of Stoic courage?", ["He escaped Nero's orders by fleeing","He denied the charges until the end","He begged Nero for a pardon in vain","He stayed calm and comforted friends"], 3, "His composure under a forced death showed the philosophy he had taught."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"He advised an emperor, grew rich, and wrote about living simply. Was Seneca a hypocrite?"});

WB.L("marcus-aurelius", "philosophy", "beginner", "Marcus Aurelius: the philosopher emperor",
  "The most powerful man in the world wrote private notes to himself about staying humble, patient and good. They became one of history's most-read books.",
  [
    ["The philosopher emperor", "Marcus Aurelius ruled Rome from AD 161 to 180 and is remembered chiefly as a philosopher.",
      "Marcus was born in AD 121 into a wealthy Roman family. Emperor Hadrian noticed him and arranged for his chosen successor, Antoninus Pius, to adopt Marcus and prepare him for power. Marcus studied intensely and loved philosophy. His Stoic tutor Junius Rusticus lent him his own copy of the teachings of Epictetus, a gift Marcus recorded with gratitude.\n\nHe became emperor in 161 and insisted on sharing power with his adoptive brother, Lucius Verus, until Verus died in 169. Historians count Marcus as the last of the 'Five Good Emperors', a period of relative stability. He is the only Roman emperor remembered mainly as a philosopher."],
    ["A troubled reign", "Marcus longed for a quiet life of study but faced war, plague and betrayal.",
      "Despite his wish for a quiet life, Marcus spent much of his reign at war. Rome fought the Parthian Empire in the east, then he spent years on the Danube frontier fighting Germanic peoples such as the Marcomanni. The Antonine Plague, probably smallpox, was brought back by returning troops and spread through the empire, killing millions by many estimates. In 175 a trusted general, Avidius Cassius, rebelled after a false rumour of Marcus's death. Several of Marcus's children died young. His writings show a man constantly trying to stay calm and decent amid pressure, loss and exhaustion."],
    ["Notes to himself", "The Meditations was a private journal of reminders, never meant for publication.",
      "Marcus wrote notes to himself in Greek, many during military campaigns. One section is headed 'Among the Quadi', a tribe he fought. We call the collection the Meditations, but the title in the manuscripts means something like 'To Himself'. These are not a polished book. They are reminders, repeated in different words, of principles he struggled to live by. That is part of their power: we're reading an emperor's honest efforts to be better.\n\nBook 2 opens by telling yourself at the start of the day that you'll meet meddling, ungrateful, arrogant people. They act this way because they don't know good from bad. We are made for cooperation, like hands or the two rows of teeth, so you can't be harmed by them or angry with them."],
    ["Key themes", "Impermanence, the present moment, duty and acceptance run through the Meditations.",
      "Marcus returns again and again to a few ideas. Everything is temporary: even emperors and empires are soon forgotten, so don't chase fame. Focus on the present task, doing it as if it were the last act of your life. Accept what happens as part of nature's order. Do your duty to others, since we are made to work together.\n\nAnd look at things as they are, stripped of grand labels: fine wine is fermented grape juice, and a purple imperial robe is sheep's wool dyed with shellfish. Seeing plainly takes the heat out of craving and envy."],
    ["Death and legacy", "Marcus's son Commodus ended Rome's golden age, but the Meditations became one of history's most-read guides to living.",
      "Marcus died in AD 180 on the Danube frontier, probably of illness. Unlike the four emperors before him, he had a surviving son, and he made Commodus his heir. Commodus proved erratic and cruel, fought as a gladiator and was assassinated in 192. Historians often mark Marcus's death as the end of Rome's golden age.\n\nOnly a few manuscripts of the Meditations survived the Middle Ages. The first printed edition appeared in 1559, and the book has been in print ever since. Readers have included Frederick the Great, the poet Matthew Arnold and Bill Clinton, along with countless ordinary people. Its appeal is simple: if the man who ruled the Roman Empire struggled with anger, grief and self-doubt, and kept trying anyway, so can the rest of us."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made Marcus Aurelius unusual among Roman emperors?", ["He refused to hold power for himself","He was the very first Roman emperor","He fought no wars during his reign","He is remembered chiefly as a philosopher"], 3, "A devoted Stoic learner, he is the only emperor remembered mainly for philosophy."],
    [1, "What did Marcus's writings reveal about his reign?", ["A man who relished war and conquest","A ruler bored by the work of governing","A man trying to stay calm through crises","A leader shielded from every hardship"], 2, "The notes show a constant effort to stay steady under pressure."],
    [2, "What was the Meditations originally?", ["A set of speeches given to the Senate","A private journal of reminders to himself","A legal code issued for the provinces","A series of letters written to his son"], 1, "Never meant for publication, it records his honest efforts to live well."],
    [3, "Why did Marcus tell himself to see things 'as they are'?", ["Plain seeing removes the pull of craving","It lets him judge other people harshly","It proved luxury was evil in itself","It helped him avoid his public duties"], 0, "Calling wine fermented grape juice strips away glamour that fuels desire."],
    [4, "Why has the Meditations appealed to readers for centuries?", ["A detailed guide to governing an empire","A record of Marcus's military victories","Accurate predictions of future events","A powerful man's honest struggle to improve"], 3, "If an emperor wrestled with the same flaws, ordinary readers can keep trying too."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The most powerful man in Rome wrote notes to himself about anger and doubt. Why?"});

WB.L("stoic-practices", "philosophy", "beginner", "Stoic practices for modern life",
  "Stoicism was always meant to be practised, not just studied. Eight exercises you can start today, most taking less than five minutes.",
  [
    ["Rehearse your day", "Preview the day's likely difficulties each morning, decide how you will respond, and hold your plans lightly.",
      "Like Marcus Aurelius, spend a few minutes each morning previewing the day. What might go wrong? A late train, a tense meeting, a child who won't get ready for school? How do you want to act if it does? This isn't pessimism. It's rehearsal, the same reason athletes visualise a race and pilots practise emergencies in simulators. Two or three situations is plenty.\n\nAdd what modern Stoics call the reserve clause. Seneca's wise person says, 'I will sail, unless something prevents me.' Commit fully to the goal, but silently add 'fate permitting'. If a storm cancels the flight, you haven't failed at what was in your control."],
    ["Widen your view", "Imagining loss, or your problem seen from far above, restores gratitude and perspective.",
      "The Stoics practised premeditatio malorum: briefly imagining the loss of your health, a loved one or your home. It sounds grim, but it often brings a rush of appreciation, pushing back against hedonic adaptation, our habit of getting used to good things. Keep it brief and gentle. If it triggers real anxiety, drop it and focus on gratitude instead.\n\nMarcus Aurelius also pictured life from above: cities, armies, weddings and funerals, all small against vast time. Try seeing your problem from a plane, then from orbit. Research led by Ethan Kross suggests that self-distancing, even talking to yourself as 'you' or by name instead of 'I', can reduce emotional reactivity and help people reason more wisely about conflicts."],
    ["Guard your conduct", "Picture a respected witness and say less, so you act on your better instincts.",
      "In his 11th letter to Lucilius, Seneca passed on a tip he borrowed from Epicurus: choose a person of fine character and live as though they were always watching. He suggested Cato, or the gentler Laelius if Cato seemed too stern. The point isn't fear of judgement. It's that a respected witness helps you act on your better instincts. When you're about to snap or cut a corner, ask what they'd do.\n\nTalk less, too. Zeno said we have two ears and one mouth so we can listen more than we speak. Epictetus urged saying only what's needed, and avoiding gossip. Before speaking, ask whether it needs saying."],
    ["Disarm insults", "An insult only wounds if you accept it as a harm, so test it for truth or answer with humour.",
      "Epictetus advised that if you're told someone has been speaking badly of you, don't defend yourself. Say, 'He obviously didn't know my other faults, or he'd have mentioned those too.' Seneca tells how Cato was struck in a public bathhouse by a man who didn't recognise him. When the man later apologised, Cato replied, 'I don't remember being hit.'\n\nThe idea is that an insult only harms you if you accept it as harm. Next time you're criticised, ask whether any of it is true. If it is, learn from it. If it isn't, it says more about them. Humour often ends the fight."],
    ["The evening review", "A calm nightly review of what went well and what to change builds the self-awareness every other practice needs.",
      "Seneca reviewed each day before sleep, asking: What bad habit did I cure today? What fault did I resist? In what way am I better? He did it without harshness, like a kind teacher, forgiving himself while noting what to change.\n\nTry writing three lines each night: one thing you did well, one thing you'd do differently, and one thing you're grateful for. It takes about five minutes. Over weeks, patterns appear, such as which situations reliably set you off. That self-awareness is the foundation of every other Stoic practice."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why preview the day's likely problems each morning?", ["It trains you to expect that everything goes wrong","It helps you avoid every difficult person","It lets you choose your response in advance","It replaces the need to plan the day at all"], 2, "Rehearsal means that when the difficult moment arrives, you've already decided how to act."],
    [1, "What does negative visualisation usually produce when done briefly and gently?", ["Renewed appreciation of what you have","Lasting dread of losing everything","Cold indifference to loved ones","A firm prediction of misfortune"], 0, "Imagining loss counters our tendency to stop noticing the good things we have."],
    [2, "Why picture a person you admire watching you?", ["It teaches you to fear public judgement","It lets you borrow their opinions on issues","It removes the need for self-control entirely","It helps you act on your better instincts"], 3, "A respected witness prompts better conduct, not fear."],
    [3, "What is the Stoic view of an insult?", ["It must always be answered at once","It only harms you if you accept it as a harm","It proves the speaker is right","It should be ignored without any reflection"], 1, "Ask whether it's true: learn from it if so, and let it go if not."],
    [4, "What is the main benefit of the evening review?", ["A record you can use against other people","Proof that you were right all day long","Self-awareness of your patterns over time","A detailed plan for the next day's meals"], 2, "A gentle nightly review reveals patterns you can then change."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Stoics rehearsed bad days on purpose. Why that made them calmer."});

WB.L("socratic-method", "philosophy", "beginner", "The Socratic method",
  "The most powerful tool Socrates left us wasn't an answer. It was a way of asking questions.",
  [
    ["Questions, not lectures", "Socrates questioned confident beliefs to test them, aiming at truth rather than victory.",
      "Socrates didn't give lectures. He asked questions. He'd find someone confident about a big idea, like justice, courage or piety, and ask them to define it. Then he'd test the definition against examples until contradictions appeared. The method is called elenchus, meaning cross-examination or testing.\n\nThe aim was not to win an argument but to get closer to the truth. Socrates said he was just as eager to be refuted as to refute, because being shown a mistake rid him of a false belief, which he saw as a benefit.\n\nHe wrote nothing himself. We know him mainly through Plato and Xenophon, and scholars still debate how much of Plato's 'Socrates' is the real man."],
    ["Testing a definition", "A counterexample can break a definition, and each failure forces a better one.",
      "At the start of Plato's Republic, the elderly Cephalus suggests that justice means telling the truth and paying back what you owe. Socrates asks: if a friend lent you a weapon, then went mad and asked for it back, would returning it be just? Clearly not. So the definition fails as stated.\n\nOthers try new definitions, such as 'helping friends and harming enemies', and each is tested in turn. Each counterexample forces a better definition. Often the dialogue ends without a final answer, but everyone understands the question more deeply than before."],
    ["Knowing you don't know", "Wisdom begins with recognising the limits of your own knowledge.",
      "In Plato's Apology, Socrates says his friend Chaerephon asked the Oracle at Delphi whether anyone was wiser than Socrates. The answer was no. Puzzled, Socrates questioned politicians, poets and craftsmen. He found they believed they knew things they didn't. The craftsmen really did know their trades, but assumed this made them experts on everything else.\n\nHe concluded he was wiser in one way only: he didn't think he knew what he didn't know. This humility is the starting point of the method. You can't learn what you think you already know."],
    ["Midwife of ideas", "Reasoning your way to an insight is uncomfortable but builds deeper, longer-lasting understanding.",
      "In Plato's Theaetetus, Socrates compares himself to a midwife, like his mother. He didn't implant ideas. He helped others bring out their own, then checked whether each 'newborn' was sound. When you reason your way to an insight, you understand it more deeply and remember it longer. Teachers use Socratic questioning to help students think rather than memorise, and law schools, especially in the United States, use it to grill students about cases.\n\nMany early dialogues end in aporia, or puzzlement. In the Meno, Socrates questions an enslaved boy about doubling a square's area. The boy gives a confident wrong answer, sees it's wrong, then finds the right one. Being shown you don't know is uncomfortable, but it makes you want to find out."],
    ["Questions to try", "Ask for meaning, evidence, assumptions, alternatives and consequences, with curiosity rather than hostility.",
      "Useful Socratic questions include: What exactly do you mean? What's your evidence? What are you assuming? Could there be another explanation? What would follow if that were true? What would someone who disagrees say? They work on news stories, workplace decisions and family debates alike. Cognitive behavioural therapists use similar questions to test anxious thoughts: 'What's the evidence this will go badly? What would I tell a friend?'\n\nQuestioning can feel like an attack if done to score points. Socrates annoyed powerful Athenians by exposing their ignorance in public, and in 399 BC he was tried and executed. Used with curiosity, it is a fine tool. Turn it on your own beliefs first."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the point of Socratic questioning?", ["To win debates by trapping opponents","To test ideas and get closer to the truth","To make others agree with you","To teach facts by repetition"], 1, "The method values being shown a mistake, because it removes a false belief."],
    [1, "Why did the example of returning a weapon to a friend who had gone mad matter?", ["It proved that justice does not really exist","It showed that friends should never lend things","It settled once and for all what justice is","'Paying back what you owe' isn't always just"], 3, "A counterexample exposes a flaw in a definition and forces a better one."],
    [2, "What made Socrates wiser than the people he questioned?", ["He didn't think he knew what he didn't know","He knew more than every expert","He had memorised the oracle's teachings","He had trained as a craftsman"], 0, "Recognising the limits of your knowledge is where learning begins."],
    [3, "Why is reasoning your way to an insight better than being told the answer?", ["It is always faster than being told","It avoids any confusion on the way","Understanding runs deeper and lasts longer","It guarantees you never make errors"], 2, "Working it out, even through puzzlement, builds lasting understanding."],
    [4, "Which question is Socratic in spirit?", ["Why are you so wrong?","Who told you that nonsense?","Can we stop talking about it?","What evidence is there for that?"], 3, "Socratic questions seek clarity and evidence, not victory."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Socrates never wrote a word, yet changed how we think. His method is one skill."});

WB.L("eastern-philosophy", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Eastern philosophy: sages of India and China",
  "At the same time Greek philosophy was flourishing, thinkers in India and China were asking similar questions, and reaching different answers.",
  [
    ["The Axial Age", "Between roughly 800 and 200 BC, major philosophical traditions arose independently across several civilisations.",
      "In 1949 the German philosopher Karl Jaspers noticed that in roughly the same period, independent traditions arose that still shape billions of lives: Greek philosophy, the Hebrew prophets, Buddhism and Jainism in India, and Confucianism and Daoism in China. Some accounts add Zoroastrianism, though its founder's dates are very uncertain.\n\nJaspers called it the Axial Age, a pivot in human history. Historians debate how tidy the idea is, and some say it overstates the coincidence. Still, across the world, thinkers began asking systematically how to live, what makes a good ruler and how to deal with suffering."],
    ["Many-sided truth", "Jain many-sidedness and Carvaka scepticism show Indian thought valuing humility and evidence.",
      "Mahavira, Jainism's great teacher, lived in north-eastern India around the time of the Buddha. Jain thinkers developed anekantavada, or many-sidedness: reality is so complex that any single statement is only partly true. The parable of the blind men and the elephant illustrates it. One feels the trunk and says it's a snake, another the leg and says it's a tree. Each is partly right; none sees the whole. For Jains, it is a reason for humility and tolerance.\n\nNot all Indian thinkers were spiritual. The Carvaka, or Lokayata, were materialists. They trusted only what we perceive directly, so they rejected the soul, rebirth, karma and the gods. Almost none of their writings survive. We know them mostly through rivals who quoted them to refute them."],
    ["Confucius and his heirs", "Confucius taught that a good society rests on virtuous people and mutual duty, and his followers debated human nature.",
      "Confucius, traditionally dated 551 to 479 BC, was a Chinese teacher and minor official in an age of war between rival states. He taught ren, humaneness, and li, proper conduct. Society works when relationships, such as ruler and subject, parent and child, and friends, are carried out with mutual responsibility. His version of the golden rule, from the Analects: 'Do not do to others what you do not want done to yourself.'\n\nHis followers disagreed on human nature. Mencius held it is basically good: anyone seeing a child about to fall into a well feels compassion. Xunzi held people are naturally selfish and need education and ritual, as a craftsman straightens wood. Confucianism later became China's official state philosophy under the Han dynasty. Exams on the Confucian classics shaped government posts for centuries, until 1905."],
    ["Laozi and the Dao", "Daoism teaches living in harmony with the Way, acting without forcing, like water.",
      "Daoism is traditionally traced to Laozi, a legendary sage whose historical existence is uncertain, and the short, poetic Dao De Jing, only about 5,000 Chinese characters. Its opening line says the Dao that can be spoken of is not the eternal Dao. The Dao, or 'the Way', is the underlying flow of the universe.\n\nDaoism values simplicity, humility and wu wei, often translated as 'effortless action': acting in harmony with things rather than forcing them, like water, which is soft yet wears down stone. The later sage Zhuangzi famously dreamt he was a butterfly, then wondered whether he was a butterfly dreaming he was Zhuangzi."],
    ["Threads and legacy", "These traditions share ideas with Stoicism and still shape cultures and practices like mindfulness.",
      "Despite big differences, these traditions share threads with each other and with Stoicism. Many warn that endless desire causes suffering and that compassion is central to a good life. Buddhism's attention to the mind resembles the Stoic focus on judgements: how we interpret events shapes how we suffer. Scholars caution against making them sound identical, since their views of the self and universe differ.\n\nTheir influence endures. Confucian values shaped government, education and family life across East Asia for over two thousand years. Buddhism is practised by hundreds of millions, including many Australians, and its meditation inspired Jon Kabat-Zinn's mindfulness-based stress reduction in the late 1970s. Daoism shaped Chinese painting, poetry, medicine and tai chi."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Karl Jaspers notice about the period 800 to 200 BC?", ["One empire came to unify all philosophy","Philosophy gradually disappeared from Asia","Greece alone produced the major thinkers","Separate traditions arose in many places"], 3, "He called it the Axial Age, though historians debate how tidy the idea is."],
    [1, "What does the parable of the blind men and the elephant illustrate?", ["Elephants are among the most dangerous animals","Each viewpoint shows only part of the truth","Our senses can never be trusted at all","The loudest person in a debate is usually right"], 1, "Many-sidedness encourages intellectual humility and tolerance of other views."],
    [2, "What did Xunzi believe about human nature, in contrast to Mencius?", ["People are naturally good and need no guidance","People's nature cannot be shaped at all","People are selfish and need education and ritual","Only rulers have a nature worth studying"], 2, "Mencius saw natural compassion; Xunzi saw a need for shaping, like straightening wood."],
    [3, "What does wu wei, or 'effortless action', mean?", ["Acting in harmony with things, not forcing them","Doing absolutely nothing at all in life","Obeying rulers without ever questioning them","Avoiding every form of effort and work"], 0, "Like water, which is soft yet wears down stone."],
    [4, "What do Buddhism and Stoicism have in common?", ["Both worship the same group of gods","Both say our interpretation shapes our suffering","Both deny that ethics really matters","Both hold identical views of self and universe"], 1, "Scholars note the parallels are real, but the traditions are not identical."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Greece wasn't alone. Why great thinkers arose across the world at about the same time."});

WB.L("enlightenment", "philosophy", "intermediate", "The Enlightenment",
  "In the 1700s, thinkers argued that reason, not tradition or kings, should guide human life. Their ideas built the modern world.",
  [
    ["Dare to know", "The Enlightenment championed reason, evidence and individual rights over tradition and unquestioned authority.",
      "In 1784 the German philosopher Immanuel Kant answered the question 'What is Enlightenment?' with a motto borrowed from the Roman poet Horace: sapere aude, 'dare to know'. He meant having the courage to use your own understanding without being guided by someone else.\n\nBuilding on the Scientific Revolution, thinkers across Europe and its colonies argued that reason and evidence, not tradition or superstition, should guide how we understand the world and organise society. Isaac Newton's Principia (1687) showed a few laws explaining both falling apples and orbiting planets. If nature followed rational laws, why not society too? The movement is usually dated from the late 1600s to around 1800."],
    ["Spreading the ideas", "Coffee houses, salons and the Encyclopédie carried Enlightenment ideas, despite repeated bans.",
      "Ideas spread through coffee houses, scientific societies, Parisian salons often hosted by women, and a booming trade in books. The great project was the Encyclopédie, edited by Denis Diderot and Jean le Rond d'Alembert and published from 1751. It aimed to gather all human knowledge, including practical trades, and French authorities repeatedly banned it.\n\nVoltaire (1694 to 1778) used wit and satire to attack intolerance and censorship. His novel Candide (1759) mocks the idea that we live in the best of all possible worlds. He was jailed in the Bastille, spent years in exile, and in 1765 helped clear Jean Calas, a Protestant wrongly executed in 1762.\n\nThe famous line 'I disapprove of what you say, but I will defend to the death your right to say it' wasn't his. His biographer Evelyn Beatrice Hall wrote it in 1906."],
    ["Rights and consent", "Locke argued governments exist to protect natural rights and rule by the consent of the governed.",
      "The English philosopher John Locke (1632 to 1704) argued that people are born with natural rights, including life, liberty and property, and that governments exist to protect them. Government rests on the consent of the governed. If it violates those rights, people may replace it.\n\nIn his Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689), he argued that the mind at birth is like a blank slate, shaped by experience rather than inborn ideas. He also made an influential case for religious toleration. His ideas deeply influenced the American Declaration of Independence, with its 'life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness', and later democratic constitutions."],
    ["Dividing power", "Montesquieu and Rousseau shaped modern ideas about checking power and grounding authority in the people.",
      "In The Spirit of the Laws (1748), Montesquieu argued liberty is safest when power is divided: one body makes laws, another carries them out, and an independent judiciary interprets them. Each checks the others, so no one becomes all-powerful. This separation of powers became central to the US Constitution. Australia's Constitution also separates legislative, executive and judicial power, and the High Court guarded judicial independence in the 1956 Boilermakers' case.\n\nJean-Jacques Rousseau opened The Social Contract (1762) with: 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.' He argued legitimate authority comes from the collective will of the people, not kings or force. Critics note his claim that dissenters can be 'forced to be free' could justify authoritarianism. Mary Wollstonecraft's Vindication of the Rights of Woman (1792) extended these arguments to women."],
    ["Science, legacy, blind spots", "Enlightenment science and ideals shaped the modern world, yet many thinkers accepted injustices.",
      "The Scottish Enlightenment produced David Hume, Adam Smith (The Wealth of Nations, 1776) and James Hutton, who argued the Earth was far older than most believed. Enlightenment science reached Australia too: James Cook's voyage, backed by the Royal Society, observed the 1769 transit of Venus from Tahiti, then charted the east coast in 1770. Joseph Banks collected thousands of plant specimens.\n\nFree speech, tolerance, democracy and equal rights are Enlightenment legacies, running through the 1948 Universal Declaration of Human Rights. But many thinkers accepted slavery, colonialism or the exclusion of women. British colonisation of Australia began in 1788, with devastating effects on Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. Many argue the answer is to apply Enlightenment principles more consistently, not abandon them."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Kant mean by 'dare to know'?", ["Having the courage to use your own understanding","Accepting what authorities teach","Studying only the natural sciences","Rejecting all earlier knowledge"], 0, "Enlightenment thinkers put reason and evidence ahead of unquestioned authority."],
    [1, "Why was the Encyclopédie significant?", ["One author wrote all of it alone","The Church sponsored and supported it","Officials repeatedly tried to ban it","It covered only the natural sciences"], 2, "Spreading knowledge widely was seen as a threat by those in power."],
    [2, "According to Locke, what is the purpose of government?", ["To enforce a single official state religion","To protect natural rights, with consent","To make citizens obey a hereditary monarch","To decide what the people may believe"], 1, "If a government violates those rights, people may replace it."],
    [3, "Why did Montesquieu favour dividing government power among branches?", ["So decisions can be made faster","So the monarch keeps full control","So voters are given fewer choices","So each branch checks the others"], 3, "Separated powers keep any one person or group from becoming all-powerful."],
    [4, "What is a fair criticism of the Enlightenment's legacy?", ["Many accepted slavery or excluded women","It rejected science and reason entirely","It opposed free speech and tolerance","It had no lasting influence on later law"], 0, "The ideals were broader than the people who first stated them."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A famous defence of free speech is usually credited to Voltaire. He never wrote it."});

WB.L("free-will", "philosophy", "advanced", "Do we have free will?",
  "If every event has a cause, including every thought in your brain, are your choices really yours? One of philosophy's oldest puzzles.",
  [
    ["The puzzle", "If every choice has prior causes, it is unclear how any choice can be free.",
      "It feels obvious that you choose freely: this morning you could have had tea instead of coffee. But science describes the world as a chain of causes. Your brain is physical matter following physical laws, shaped by genes, upbringing and experiences you didn't choose. If every decision was caused by earlier events, stretching back before you were born, in what sense could you have done otherwise?\n\nThis is the problem of free will and determinism. It matters because praise, blame, pride, guilt and justice all seem to assume that people could have chosen differently."],
    ["Determinism or libertarianism", "Hard determinists deny free will, while libertarians say some choices are not fully caused.",
      "Hard determinists say every choice is fully caused by earlier events, so free will in the deep sense doesn't exist. The feeling of choosing is real, but produced by brain processes we don't control. In 1814 Pierre-Simon Laplace imagined an intellect that knew every particle's position and could predict the entire future. Neuroscientist Robert Sapolsky, in his 2023 book Determined, argues this should make us rethink blame and favour prevention and rehabilitation over retribution.\n\nPhilosophical 'libertarians', nothing to do with the political kind, hold that some choices aren't fully determined and we originate our actions. Some point to quantum randomness. Critics reply that randomness gives no control: a choice caused by a random neuron event isn't more 'yours'."],
    ["Acting on your reasons", "You act freely when you act on your own reasons without coercion, even if those reasons were caused.",
      "Most professional philosophers are compatibilists: in the large 2020 PhilPapers survey, a clear majority leaned that way. They argue free will doesn't require escaping cause and effect. You act freely when you act on your own desires, reasons and values, without being forced, manipulated or compelled. If you choose coffee because you want coffee, that's free, even if your wanting was caused. If someone holds a gun to your head, it isn't.\n\nDavid Hume in the 1700s, and more recently the late Daniel Dennett, defended versions of this view. The ancient Stoics were early compatibilists. Chrysippus said a pushed cylinder rolls because of its own shape: events prompt us, but our response comes from our own character."],
    ["Frankfurt's device", "Responsibility may not require that you could have done otherwise.",
      "In 1969 Harry Frankfurt challenged a common assumption: that you're only responsible if you could have done otherwise. Imagine a scientist secretly places a device in Jones's brain. If Jones is about to decide not to vote for a certain candidate, the device will make him vote for them anyway. But Jones decides to vote for them entirely on his own, and the device never activates.\n\nJones couldn't have done otherwise, yet he seems fully responsible, because the choice came from his own reasons. Frankfurt's cases gave compatibilists a powerful argument, though debate continues."],
    ["What brain science shows", "Libet's experiments are debated rather than decisive, and views on free will shape how we treat responsibility.",
      "In the early 1980s, Benjamin Libet asked people to flex a wrist whenever they felt like it and note when they felt the urge. A build-up of brain activity, the readiness potential, began several hundred milliseconds before people reported deciding. Some concluded the brain decides before 'we' do. A 2012 study led by Aaron Schurger suggested it may partly reflect random background fluctuations instead. Libet himself argued people could still 'veto' an action. The experiments are debated, not decisive.\n\nThe debate matters in practice. Courts already weigh age, mental impairment and duress. If choices are shaped by circumstances we didn't choose, perhaps we should be more compassionate and humbler about success. Yet most people think it still makes sense to hold ourselves responsible. Build habits and surroundings that make good choices easier."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does determinism threaten the idea of free will?", ["Science proves that people never make decisions","Laws forbid people from choosing anything freely","Earlier causes make 'doing otherwise' unclear","Brains are unable to process real choices"], 2, "Our ideas of blame and praise assume we could have chosen differently."],
    [1, "Why do critics say quantum randomness doesn't rescue libertarian free will?", ["Quantum effects never occur in nature","Random events are fully predictable","Randomness is the same as determinism","Chance gives you no more control"], 3, "A choice caused by chance isn't any more 'yours' than one caused by earlier events."],
    [2, "According to compatibilists, when do you act freely?", ["Only when your choice has no prior cause at all","When you act on your own reasons, uncoerced","Only when you have unlimited options to pick from","Whenever you simply feel that you want something"], 1, "Free will doesn't need escaping cause and effect, only freedom from force or manipulation."],
    [3, "What does Frankfurt's thought experiment suggest about Jones?", ["He is responsible, because the choice came from his own reasons","He is not responsible, because he couldn't have done otherwise","He is not responsible, because the device exists","He can't be judged either way"], 0, "Being able to do otherwise may not be required for responsibility."],
    [4, "What do Libet's experiments show about free will?", ["That conscious choice is a complete illusion","That people can never veto an action","Nothing decisive, as the findings are debated","That decisions take exactly one second"], 2, "Later research questioned the original interpretation of the readiness potential."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your brain may start a movement before you feel you chose it. What that means."});

WB.L("existentialism", "philosophy", "intermediate", "Existentialism",
  "No script, no predetermined purpose. Existentialists argued we're free to create meaning, and responsible for what we make of ourselves.",
  [
    ["Existence before essence", "Humans have no preset purpose: we exist first, then create ourselves through our choices.",
      "In a famous 1945 lecture in Paris, Jean-Paul Sartre summed up existentialism as 'existence precedes essence'. A paper knife has a purpose in the craftsman's mind before it is made, so its essence comes first. Humans, Sartre argued, have no pre-set purpose. We exist first, then create who we are through our choices and actions. For Sartre, an atheist, this followed from the absence of a creator.\n\nSo we're 'condemned to be free': we can't escape choosing, and even refusing to choose is a choice. With freedom comes total responsibility, and anxiety. Many flee through 'bad faith', blaming their job, upbringing or society. In Being and Nothingness (1943), Sartre described a café waiter whose movements are a little too perfect, as if hiding his freedom behind a role."],
    ["Roots and fame", "Kierkegaard and Nietzsche laid the foundations, and postwar Paris made existentialism a cultural movement.",
      "Søren Kierkegaard (1813 to 1855) focused on the individual facing anxiety and choice, and on a passionate commitment that reason can't justify, for him Christian faith: a 'leap of faith'. Friedrich Nietzsche (1844 to 1900) declared in 1882 that 'God is dead', meaning belief in God was fading in Europe and leaving a crisis of values. He feared nihilism and urged people to create their own values and affirm life, even its suffering.\n\nAfter the war and the occupation of France, Sartre, Simone de Beauvoir and their circle held court in Left Bank cafés like the Café de Flore and founded the journal Les Temps modernes in 1945. Sartre was offered the 1964 Nobel Prize in Literature and declined it, saying a writer shouldn't become an institution."],
    ["Camus and the absurd", "Camus argued life is absurd, but we can rebel by living fully anyway.",
      "Albert Camus, a French writer born in Algeria in 1913, rejected the existentialist label but is closely linked to it. He described the absurd: the clash between our longing for meaning and a silent, indifferent universe. His novel The Stranger (1942) follows a man who refuses to pretend to feelings he doesn't have.\n\nIn The Myth of Sisyphus, published the same year, he retold the Greek myth of a man condemned to push a boulder uphill forever. Camus concluded: 'One must imagine Sisyphus happy.' Embracing life in full awareness of the absurd is a kind of rebellion. He won the Nobel Prize in 1957."],
    ["De Beauvoir's freedom", "De Beauvoir applied existentialism to women's lives and argued that true freedom means willing freedom for others.",
      "Simone de Beauvoir, a philosopher, novelist and Sartre's lifelong partner, wrote The Second Sex (1949), a founding text of modern feminism. She argued that women had been defined as 'the Other' in relation to men, treated as secondary rather than as free subjects, and that ideas of femininity are shaped by society, not fixed by nature. Her famous line, 'One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman', applied the existentialist idea that we're not bound by a fixed essence.\n\nIn The Ethics of Ambiguity (1947), she argued that true freedom requires willing freedom for others too."],
    ["Quarrels and living it", "Existentialism had critics and bitter quarrels, but its practical message is authenticity and responsibility.",
      "Religious thinkers objected to Sartre's atheism, Marxists thought it too individual, and others found it gloomy. Its leaders quarrelled too. Sartre and Camus were friends until 1952, when a harsh review of Camus's The Rebel in Sartre's journal caused a bitter split. Camus had condemned political violence and Soviet labour camps, while Sartre, for a time, defended the Soviet Union. They never reconciled before Camus died in a car accident in 1960.\n\nThe practical message is demanding but empowering. Live authentically, by values you've chosen rather than what others expect. Take responsibility instead of hiding behind 'I had no choice'. Accept that some anxiety comes with freedom. Create meaning through commitments to people, work and causes. Viktor Frankl's logotherapy and modern existential psychotherapy draw on these ideas."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does 'existence precedes essence' mean?", ["We are all born with one fixed purpose","Our essence is decided in advance by a creator","We exist first, then define ourselves","Nothing really exists beyond our thoughts"], 2, "With no preset human nature, we are what we make of ourselves."],
    [1, "What is 'bad faith' in Sartre's sense?", ["Pretending you have no choice","Doubting your own religious beliefs","Telling a lie to a close friend","Breaking a promise made to someone"], 0, "Blaming job, upbringing or society hides our freedom."],
    [2, "What did Nietzsche mean by 'God is dead'?", ["God's non-existence had been scientifically proven","Religion ought to be banned from public life","People had become more virtuous and moral","Belief in God was fading, creating a values crisis"], 3, "He feared nihilism and urged people to create their own values."],
    [3, "How does Camus suggest we respond to the absurd?", ["Search for a hidden cosmic meaning behind it","Live fully, aware of it, as a kind of rebellion","Give up on life because nothing matters","Pretend the universe has a plan for us"], 1, "'One must imagine Sisyphus happy.'"],
    [4, "What does de Beauvoir's 'One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman' express?", ["Biology fully determines a woman's role in life","Gender has no real effect on social life","Femininity is shaped by society, not nature","Women should avoid defining themselves at all"], 2, "It applies the existentialist idea that we are not bound by a fixed essence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Sartre turned down a Nobel Prize. His reason says a lot about the man."});

